<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kānāwai Māmalahoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Law of the Splintered Paddle, Law of the Splintered Oar, Kānāwai hoʻōla kanaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kānāwai Māmalahoe, or Law of the Splintered Paddle, is a precept of Hawaiian law proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> in 1797, ordering that the old, women and children be free to lie by the roadside without fear of harm. It arose from an incident in Puna, where a fisherman, Kaleleiki, struck Kamehameha on the head with a paddle that broke, yet spared him; years later Kamehameha ruled that the man had only defended his land and family. Drawn from older Hawaiian ideas of legitimate rule, the law is written into Article 9, Section 10 of the state constitution and has become a model for modern human rights law on the treatment of civilians in war.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His <b>Law of the Splintered Paddle</b>, the Kānāwai Māmalahoe, which protected the weak and travellers on the road, is now written into the constitution of the State of Hawaii.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1790s Kamehameha pardoned a fisherman who had struck him while defending his family, and proclaimed the <b>Law of the Splintered Paddle</b>: old people, women and children might lie by the roadside in safety.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
